## Experiment
Topic: Topic Can test generation, policy-equivalence checking, and canonicalization be separated so that an independently written parser and verifier validate every canonical archive? _(auto-enqueued follow-up from topic-can-the-original-executable-source-code-ex)_
Goal: Generate 128 finite-domain policy archives, process them with one reference canonicalizer and 30 deterministic lossy mutants, and use an independently implemented parser plus exhaustive semantic verifier to test whether structural acceptance and idempotence fail to detect policy changes.

Protocol to implement:
1. Use Python 3.11 or newer with only the standard library and matplotlib. Fix the corpus seed to 20250308. Record the Python and matplotlib versions in results.json.
2. Implement four source modules with the following dependency rule: testgen.py generates source archives and must not import canonicalizer.py or verifier.py; canonicalizer.py contains its own archive/policy parser and the reference plus mutant canonicalizers; independent_parser.py must not import canonicalizer.py and must independently parse and validate archives; verifier.py may import only independent_parser.py and must implement policy evaluation independently of canonicalizer.py. Add a static test that fails if independent_parser.py or verifier.py imports canonicalizer.py.
3. Define the complete request domain as principals [p0,p1,p2,p3], actions [a0,a1,a2,a3], resources [r0,r1,r2,r3], MFA values [false,true], hours [0,1,2,3], and zones [internal,external]. Exhaustive verification therefore evaluates exactly 4*4*4*2*4*2 = 1024 requests per policy.
4. Define policy JSON as an object with optional default_effect and optional rules. default_effect is allow or deny and defaults to deny; rules defaults to an empty list. Each rule has a required unique string id and optional effect, principals, actions, resources, mfa, hours, zones, and priority. effect defaults to deny. principals/actions/resources/hours/zones each default to the complete corresponding domain and, when present, must be nonempty arrays without duplicates. mfa is any, required, or forbidden and defaults to any. priority is an integer from 0 through 3 and defaults to 0. Reject unknown keys, wrong types, out-of-domain values, duplicate IDs, duplicate array elements, and non-UTF-8 JSON.
5. Define policy semantics independently in verifier.py. A rule matches when all scalar request components belong to its selector arrays and its MFA condition is satisfied. Among matching rules, retain those having the greatest priority. The result is deny if any retained rule has effect deny, otherwise allow. If no rule matches, return default_effect. Rule IDs have no semantic effect.
6. Represent every archive as a ZIP file containing exactly one entry named policy.json. Write it with ZIP_STORED, timestamp (1980,1,1,0,0,0), create_system=3, external_attr=(0o100644 << 16), no archive comment, and no entry extra field or comment. Encode JSON as UTF-8 using json.dumps with sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(',',':'), followed by one newline. Reject archives with extra entries, duplicate entry names, directories, traversal names, encryption, compression other than ZIP_STORED, or a policy.json larger than 1 MiB.
7. In testgen.py, generate 112 random policies using random.Random(20250308). For each policy choose default_effect uniformly; choose 2 through 6 rules uniformly; choose each rule effect uniformly; choose each selector by drawing a uniformly random nonzero bit mask over its domain; choose mfa uniformly from any/required/forbidden; and choose priority uniformly from 0 through 3. Use IDs r00, r01, and so on. If two generated rules are identical except for ID, redraw the latter rule.
8. Add 16 deterministic edge policies to make a 128-policy corpus: (E01) no rules/default deny; (E02) no rules/default allow; (E03) one restrictive allow rule/default deny; (E04) one restrictive deny rule/default allow; (E05) one allow rule whose five array selectors are all complete/default deny; (E06) one deny rule whose five array selectors are all complete/default allow; (E07) two disjoint allow rules/default deny; (E08) two disjoint deny rules/default allow; (E09) two rules with identical selectors, allow priority 3 and deny priority 0; (E10) identical selectors, deny priority 3 and allow priority 0; (E11) three rules where only the third matches request (p3,a3,r3,true,3,external); (E12) four rules with alternating effects and priorities 0,1,2,3; (E13) one allow rule with principals/actions/resources/hours containing their first two values and zones complete, mfa required; (E14) the E13 selectors with effect deny, default allow, and mfa forbidden; (E15) two same-effect rules with singleton selectors (p0,a0,r0,false,0,internal) and (p1,a1,r1,true,1,external); (E16) the E15 construction with the opposite effect and opposite default. For E03 and E04 use the singleton request (p1,a1,r1,true,1,external). For E07 use singleton rules for (p0,a0,r0,false,0,internal) and (p1,a1,r1,true,1,external); E08 is analogous. Any selector not explicitly specified in E09-E12 is the complete domain, and mfa is any; in E11 the first two rules must not match the stated p3 request. Assign edge-rule IDs in list order. Write all 128 source archives through testgen.py's own archive writer.
9. Implement a reference canonicalizer C0 in canonicalizer.py. Its private parser applies all schema defaults. It sorts selector arrays in the domain orders from step 3, preserves rule order, and emits every top-level and rule field explicitly using the deterministic archive format in step 6.
10. Implement exactly 30 mutant canonicalizers with IDs D01-D09, F01-F07, T01-T08, and M01-M06. Assign seeds 730001 through 730030 in that ID order and record them, although no runtime randomness is permitted after construction. Every mutant first parses and normalizes exactly as C0, applies its listed transformation to every applicable occurrence, and writes deterministically. D01 omits default_effect; D02 omits every rule effect; D03-D07 omit respectively principals, actions, resources, hours, and zones from every rule; D08 omits every mfa; D09 omits every priority. Omission invokes the schema defaults when reparsed. F01 sets default_effect to allow; F02 sets every rule effect to allow; F03-F05 set respectively every principals, actions, and resources array to its first singleton [p0], [a0], or [r0]; F06 sets every mfa to required; F07 sets every priority to 0. T01 retains only the first rule; T02 retains only the first two rules; T03-T07 retain only the first element of every principals, actions, resources, hours, and zones array respectively; T08 replaces every priority x with min(x,1). M01 groups rules by effect and emits at most two rules in deny-then-allow order: for each effect, union each array selector, set mfa to the common value if all members agree and otherwise any, set priority to the maximum member priority, and use ID merged_deny or merged_allow. M02-M06 replace respectively every principals, actions, resources, hours, or zones array having at least two elements with that field's complete domain; interpret this as merging the listed values into a wildcard. Mutants other than D01-D09 emit all fields explicitly. These transformations are intended to be deterministic and idempotent; do not add special-case code that forces the tests to pass.
11. Run independent_parser.py on all 128 source archives. Require all to be accepted before continuing. Run C0 on every source archive and run all 30 mutants on every source archive, producing 128 baseline outputs and 3840 mutant outputs.
12. For structural acceptance, parse each of the 3968 output archives using independent_parser.py. Count an archive as structurally accepted only if ZIP validation, JSON parsing, schema validation, default application, and unique-rule-ID validation all succeed.
13. For fixed-point testing, apply the same canonicalizer a second time to its own output and compare the first- and second-pass archive bytes using exact byte equality. Perform this for C0 and every mutant on every corpus policy. Also compute SHA-256 hashes to report mismatches, but use direct byte equality as the decision criterion.
14. For exact policy-equivalence testing, parse each original source archive and each canonicalized output only through independent_parser.py. In verifier.py enumerate all 1024 requests in lexicographic product order and compare allow/deny results. An output is equivalent only if all 1024 results match. Record the number and fraction of differing requests and the lexicographically first counterexample request for every inequivalent output.
15. Define a mutant as semantically rejected when at least one of its 128 outputs is not equivalent to the corresponding source policy. Define C0 as correct only if all 128 C0 outputs are structurally accepted, byte-idempotent, and exactly equivalent to their sources.
16. Repeat the complete run once in a fresh temporary output directory in the same process, excluding wall-clock timestamps and environment metadata, and require identical source archives, canonical outputs, equivalence matrices, and aggregate counts. This is the run-level determinism check.
17. Save results.csv with one row per canonicalizer-policy pair and columns canonicalizer_id, seed, policy_id, structural_accept, fixed_point, equivalent, differing_requests, differing_fraction, and first_counterexample. Save results.json with aggregate counts, versions, runtime, and the final decision.
18. Produce key_result.png at 1800 by 900 pixels. Plot three bars with heights equal to the number of mutants satisfying: structural acceptance on all 128 policies, fixed-point equality on all 128 policies, and semantic rejection on at least one policy. Use a y-axis from 0 to 30, label each bar with its integer count, and draw a dashed horizontal threshold at 27 over the semantic-rejection bar. Add a caption stating whether C0 passed all baseline checks.
19. Report the hypothesis as SUPPORTED if and only if C0 passes all checks, all 30 mutants have structural acceptance on all 128 outputs, all 30 mutants have byte-identical fixed points on all 128 outputs, at least 27 of 30 mutants are semantically rejected, and the repeated run is identical. Report REFUTED otherwise, including which condition failed.
20. Measure elapsed wall-clock time around steps 11-18 using time.perf_counter. Fail the resource requirement if elapsed time exceeds 30 minutes; the expected workload is approximately 4.1 million request evaluations for mutants plus baseline checks and should normally finish well below the limit.

Metrics to compute: Baseline correctness: whether C0 has 128/128 structurally accepted outputs, 128/128 byte-identical fixed points, and 128/128 exact policy-equivalence passes., Mutant structural acceptance rate: accepted mutant outputs divided by 3840; the hypothesis requires 3840/3840 and, at mutant level, 30/30 mutants accepted on every policy., Mutant fixed-point rate: first-pass outputs exactly equal to second-pass outputs divided by 3840; the hypothesis requires 3840/3840 and 30/30 mutants fixed on every policy., Semantic rejection count: number of the 30 mutants having at least one inequivalent output under exhaustive enumeration of all 1024 requests; the support threshold is at least 27., Archive-level semantic inequivalence rate: number of inequivalent mutant-policy pairs divided by 3840., Behavioral distance: for each mutant-policy pair, differing request outcomes divided by 1024; report per-mutant mean, median, maximum, and number of nonzero distances., Determinism: exact equality of generated archives, canonical outputs, equivalence matrices, and aggregate counts across two repeated runs; required to be 100%., Wall-clock runtime: elapsed minutes for parsing, canonicalization, verification, reporting, and figure generation; required to be at most 30 minutes.
Expected if hypothesis holds: C0 passes all 128 structural, fixed-point, and equivalence checks; all 3840 mutant outputs are accepted by the independent parser; all 3840 mutant outputs are byte-idempotent; at least 27 of the 30 mutants, corresponding to a rejection proportion of at least 0.90, have one or more exhaustive finite-domain counterexamples; repeated runs are identical; and key_result.png shows bars at 30 for structural acceptance, 30 for fixed points, and at least 27 for semantic rejection.
Expected if hypothesis fails: The hypothesis is refuted if C0 fails its baseline, any mutant fails structural acceptance on any policy, any mutant fails byte-level fixed-point equality on any policy, 26 or fewer mutants are semantically rejected, the repeated runs differ, or the experiment exceeds 30 minutes.
Wall-clock budget: about 30 minutes. Keep the experiment inside it; if
